WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.259
Why read the critics.

2
00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:05.613
If the last lesson’s speakers are right, very capable AI is close.

3
00:00:05.893 --> 00:00:13.104
The people in this lesson think that view is wrong in one of two ways, and the distinction is the most useful idea in the lesson.

4
00:00:13.384 --> 00:00:23.176
Capability critiques say the current approach cannot reach some level of ability: human-level reasoning, reliable autonomy, understanding of the physical world.

5
00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.141
These are claims about the ceiling.

6
00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:35.683
Impact critiques say that whatever the capabilities, effects on the economy and society will be slower, more uneven, and more controllable than the timelines suggest.

7
00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:37.712
These are claims about diffusion.

8
00:00:37.992 --> 00:00:39.379
The two can come apart.

9
00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:47.936
Benchmarks can soar while job markets barely move, and that combination would vindicate impact critics while embarrassing capability critics.

10
00:00:48.216 --> 00:00:50.797
Keep them separate when you read, and when you score.

11
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:53.765
A second reason to read the critics is professional.

12
00:00:54.045 --> 00:00:58.909
When you lead a team, the critics’ arguments are the failure modes you need to design against.

13
00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:04.587
Marcus’s reliability critique is the reason for every validator you wrote in Studio 1.

14
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:11.096
LeCun’s planning critique is the reason agents need checkpoints and human approval in Studio 4.

15
00:01:11.376 --> 00:01:15.024
You do not have to agree with their forecasts to use their objections.
